## 4. Design and Analysis

### A. Difference between Prefix and Postfix

| Prefix (++i) | Postfix (i++) |
|---|---|
| Increments first | Returns old value first |
| Returns reference | Returns a copy |
| Uses no parameter | Uses an int dummy parameter |
| Generally faster | Generally slower |

The compiler identifies prefix and postfix using the dummy `int` parameter.

```cpp
Time& operator++();       // Prefix
Time operator++(int);     // Postfix
```

### B. Why does postfix return a copy?

Postfix must preserve the original value before incrementing. Therefore, it creates and returns a temporary copy.

Prefix directly increments the object and returns the same object by reference.

### C. Why are two versions of operator[] needed?

- Non-const version returns `string&`, allowing modification of a track.
- Const version returns `const string&`, allowing reading without modification.

### D. What happens when an index is out of range?

The program checks whether the index is negative or greater than or equal to the playlist size.

If the index is invalid, it throws an `out_of_range` exception instead of accessing invalid memory.

### E. Minute and Hour Rollover

When the time reaches `59:59`, the next increment changes it to `1:00:00`.

This is handled by checking whether seconds and minutes reach 60.

