Postfix increment creates a copy of the old object, whereas prefix increment returns the updated object.

## 8. Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis

Instead of using a checked `operator[]`, playlist access can use an `at()` function or an unchecked subscript operator.

**A. Using at()**

```cpp
string& getTrack(int i) {
    return tracks.at(i);
}
```

`at()` automatically checks the index and throws an exception if it is invalid.

**B. Using unchecked operator[]**

```cpp
string& getTrack(int i) {
    return tracks[i];
}
```

This does not perform range checking. An invalid index can cause undefined behaviour.

### Comparison

| Method | Range Check | Time Complexity |
|---|---|---|
| Checked operator[] | Yes | O(1) |
| at() | Yes | O(1) |
| Unchecked operator[] | No | O(1) |

Checked access performs an additional comparison before accessing the track.

### Measuring 10,000,000 Playlist Accesses

The time taken by checked and unchecked access can be measured using `chrono` with 10,000,000 accesses. Record the actual time for both methods.

**Conclusion:** All three methods have O(1) time complexity. Checked access is safer, while unchecked access avoids the additional range check.

